<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gambhir Singh</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1788">1788</a>–<a href="/years/1834">1834</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gambhir Singh was a prince of the Manipuri royal house who led the Manipur Levy, raised with British help, to drive the Burmese out of Manipur during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-anglo-burmese-war">First Anglo-Burmese War</a>. He was restored as raja in 1826 under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-yandabo">Treaty of Yandabo</a> and recovered the Kabaw valley region for a time.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gambhir-singh.jpg" alt="Gambhir Singh" width="320" height="430" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Moirangthemcha · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gambhirsingh.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Gambhir Singh, also known as Chinglen Nongdrenkhomba, was a son of Bhagya Chandra of Manipur. Four of his brothers, Labanyachandra, Madhuchandra, Chourajit Singh and Marjit Singh, ruled before him. When Marjit Singh seized the throne from Chourajit with Burmese help, Chourajit fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cachar">Cachar</a>, and Gambhir Singh went with him as a loyal lieutenant.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cachar">Cachar</a> the brothers dethroned its king, Govinda Chandra. Refused protection by the British East India Company, Govinda Chandra asked the King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> to restore him, and in 1823 a large Burmese army entered Cachar to seize the three brothers, advancing up to the Company's territory.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In 1823 the British opened communications with Gambhir Singh, and 500 Meiteis under his command were taken into British pay. Known first as the Gambhir Singh Levy and later as the Manipur Levy, the force fought beside British troops after Lord Amherst, the Governor General of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a>, declared war on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> in March 1824. Under Gambhir Singh and his second cousin Nara Singh it played a key part in driving the Burmese out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cachar">Cachar</a> and Manipur. By Article 2 of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-yandabo">Treaty of Yandabo</a>, signed on 24 February 1826, Gambhir Singh was recognised as independent ruler of Manipur.</p><p>According to the Khaki Ngamba, in April or May 1829 he was passing through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sylhet">Sylhet</a> with a British expedition against the Khasis when the Muslim Muharram processions and the Hindu Rathajatra fell on the same day. The Faujdar of Sylhet, Ganar Khan, ordered the Hindu community to put off their procession, and clashes broke out. Gambhir Singh, a devout Vaishnava, sent his Manipuri troops to restore order and protect the Hindu worshippers, and the procession went ahead. Local tradition remembers him as a protector of the religious practices of the Hindu community of Sylhet.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Gambhir Singh is credited with bringing the Rathajatra festival and the worship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jagannath">Jagannath</a> to Manipur after he took part in the festival at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sylhet">Sylhet</a>. He reigned until his death on 9 January 1834 and was succeeded by his infant son Chandra Kirti, with Nara Singh, later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharaja">Maharaja</a>, as regent.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gambhir Singh” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Burmese occupation from 1819 was ended in 1826 by <b>Gambhir Singh</b> with British help.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-manipur">Kingdom of Manipur</a></li></ul></section>
